/// Chapter 24 of the guide, written from md-docs/24-testing.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page TestingGuide(path: "/docs/guide/testing", title: "Testing", description: "wf test: render tests with snapshots, seeding stores, tests that click and type in headless Chrome, wf verify, and running them in CI.", layout: DocsShell("Testing", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "24" }), blurb: "Tests written in WebFluent: render a component and read what it shows, or click through it in a real browser — then check every page in one.", source: "md-docs/24-testing.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "a-test-that-looks", label: "A test that looks"), Anchor(id: "a-test-that-acts", label: "A test that acts"), Anchor(id: "seeding-data-and-stores", label: "Seeding data and stores"), Anchor(id: "running-them", label: "Running them"), Anchor(id: "checking-every-page-wf-verify", label: "Checking every page: wf verify"), Anchor(id: "in-ci", label: "In CI"), Anchor(id: "what-to-test", label: "What to test")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/security", prevLabel: "23. {t("ch.23")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/project-structure", nextLabel: "25. {t("ch.25")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("Tests are WebFluent declarations: `test \"name\" \{ … \}`. A test renders something — a component, a fragment of a page — and holds it to what it must and must not show. A test that clicks or types runs in a real, headless browser. `wf verify` then loads every page of the built site in one.")

        Heading("A test that looks", id: "a-test-that-looks").h2

        Markdown("A test declaration renders a body with the project's components, stores, types and constants at hand, and holds the result to what it expects:")

        CodeBlock(#"component PriceTag(_ amount: Number, sale: Bool = false) {
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Text(format(amount, .currency)).bold
        if sale { Badge("Sale").danger }
    }
}

test "a price tag shows the amount as currency" {
    PriceTag(1234.5)
    expect "$1,234.50"
    expect not "Sale"
}

test "a sale tag carries the badge" {
    PriceTag(10, sale: true)
    expect "Sale"
}

test "a list renders each row"(data: { rows: [{ name: "a" }, { name: "b" }] }) {
    for r in rows { Text(r.name) }
    expect "a"
    expect "b"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- Tests live in `tests/*.wf` (or beside the code in `src/`).\n- `expect \"text\"` must appear in what the page shows; `expect not \"text\"` must not.\n- `data: \{ … \}` supplies values by name; a `state` in the body is seeded with its initial value.\n- Each render is also compared to `tests/__snapshots__/<file>/<test>.html`, written when missing; `--update` rewrites them after an intended change.\n\n`wf test` exits non-zero on a failure, with a diff.")

        Heading("A test that acts", id: "a-test-that-acts").h2

        Markdown("Everything above only **looks**: the body is rendered and the expects read the result. A click cannot be rendered — it runs a handler, which runs an action, which changes a store, which repaints — so a test with an interaction in it is compiled into a real page, served, and run in a headless browser. Same compiler, same runtime, same output a reader gets.")

        CodeBlock(#"test "reporting an incident opens one more" {
    use IncidentStore
    state draft = ""
    Text("{IncidentStore.open} open")
    Input(bind: draft, label: "What happened")
    Button("Report") { on click { IncidentStore.report(draft) } }

    expect "2 open"
    type "Queue backing up" into "What happened"
    click "Report"
    expect "3 open"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Table(caption: "A test that acts") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Step").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`expect \"text\"` · `expect not \"text\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("What the page must, or must not, show")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`click \"Save\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Clicks whatever carries that name")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`type \"Ada\" into \"Name\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Types into the control that label names")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`press \"Enter\"` · `press \"Escape\" in \"Search\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("A key, on the focused element or a named one")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The steps run **in the order written** — that is the whole meaning, since what a click did is only visible in the expect that follows it.\n\nEverything is found the way a reader finds it: by the name on it. A button is its text; a control is its label, its placeholder or its `aria-label`. That is the same name the accessibility checks hold a page to, so a test that passes here is a page somebody can use — and a test that cannot find what it asks for says so:")

        CodeBlock(#"  FAIL  tests/cart.wf — the count follows what is added
        nothing to click called "Publish"
        the page showed:
        Nothing here"#, title: "")

        Markdown("**A handler that throws fails the test**, even where the expects would have passed — a click that quietly did nothing is the bug, not a pass. These tests need a Chrome or Chromium on the machine, or `WF_CHROME` pointing at one; a test that only looks needs neither.\n\nA test that acts takes no snapshot. What it expects is the test.")

        Heading("Seeding data and stores", id: "seeding-data-and-stores").h2

        Markdown("A test's `data:` names values its body reads, and a key that is a store's name seeds that store's state:")

        CodeBlock(#"test "the cart shows what is in it"(data: { Cart: { items: [{ id: "a", qty: 2 }] } }) {
    CartSummary()
    expect "2 items"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Running them", id: "running-them").h2

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf test"),
                Line(command: "wf test tests/cart.wf"),
                Line(command: "wf test --update")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf test` runs every test under `tests/` (and any declared in `src/`), prints a line per test and exits non-zero when one fails — so it is a CI step as it is. `--update` rewrites the snapshots after a change you meant. A build never includes tests.\n\nA test that acts needs Chrome or Chromium on the machine, or `WF_CHROME` pointing at one; a test that only looks needs neither.")

        Heading("Checking every page: wf verify", id: "checking-every-page-wf-verify").h2

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build && wf verify")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf verify` serves the build, opens every route in headless Chrome, and fails on an uncaught exception, a `console.error`, a request that failed, an image that did not load, or a page that rendered no text. It prints each route's first contentful paint, its size and its requests; `--budget 400` fails a route slower than 400 ms to paint, and `--json` is the report for a pipeline. It also lists the built-ins no page drew — a fact about coverage, not a failure. [CLI](/docs/guide/cli#wf-verify) has the details.")

        Heading("In CI", id: "in-ci").h2

        CodeBlock(#"name: Check
on: [push, pull_request]
jobs:
  check:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash
      - run: echo "$HOME/.webfluent/bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
      - run: wf fmt --check
      - run: wf build
      - run: wf test
      - run: wf verify --budget 1000"#, title: "yaml")

        Markdown("GitHub's Ubuntu runners come with Chrome, so the interactive tests and `wf verify` run there as they do on your machine.")

        Heading("What to test", id: "what-to-test").h2

        Markdown("- **Components** with more than one state: each branch a prop can take.\n- **Stores** through what a component shows of them, seeded with `data:`.\n- **Flows** a reader depends on — signing in, adding to a cart, submitting a form — as tests that act.\n- **Every page** with `wf verify`, on every build.\n\nThe type checker and the accessibility lints already catch a large class of mistakes; tests are for behaviour they cannot see.")
    }
}
